From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 08:38:03 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: add the FRITZ!Box 7590's fifth GPHY

The WAN PHY: silicon id 0x0F on RCU reset bit 31, reset-name "phyF".

This is a device-tree-only change. xrx500_phy_fw.c has carried the fifth
table entry since the port began - RCU_RD_GPHYF_XRX500 = BIT(31) with
LBADR 0x268 at index 4 - and seale_avm.dtsi already sizes the aperture
to 0x280, which covers LBADR/MBADR at 0x268/0x26C. Only the three DT
properties were missing.

Bit 31 was the open question. On the GRX350 it behaves as the GPHY-group
master reset: toggling it topples the clock group the four GE cores have
just booted from and they revert to ROM. On the GRX550 it is not that.
The vendor device tree's phy-xrx500@3c00 names five reset lines
phy0..phy4 on bits 29/28/26/25/31, with bit 30 separately claimed as
intel,global-reset by the reset-controller node itself, so bit 31 is
GPHY4's own line and the group-master reading does not carry over. The
bits are also non-contiguous and out of order - 29, 28, 26, 25, 31, 27
unused - so no closed-form expression can generate them.

The second cell is the RST_STAT status bit and is set to 31 to match.
Its semantics are unvalidated on GRX550, and RST_STAT bit 31 doubles as
the watchdog reset cause, so a poll of it could observe the watchdog's
bit rather than the PHY's. It cannot happen on this path. The
firmware loader acquires the handle only so the named-line lookup
succeeds, then discards it - xrx500_phy_fw.c:257, "(void)phy_rst" - and
drives RCU_RST_REQ by direct read-modify-write at :297 (assert) and :322
(deassert), taking the bit from xrx500_gphy[] rather than from the DT.
reset_control_status() is the only reader of the status op in the reset
core (core.c:631-641); no assert, deassert, reset, acquire or release
path consults it, and our devm_reset_control_get is EXCLUSIVE, whose
devres release only calls reset_control_put. Nothing in drivers/net
calls reset_control_status at all. So the cell is consumed solely by the
provider's "< nr_resets" bounds check (reset-lantiq.c:152-164), which 31
passes, and by the shift into an opaque dedupe key. Worth noting for
anyone tempted to modernise the loader onto the reset framework: doing
so would make the poll live, and lantiq_rcu_reset_update() would then
spin on that bit for up to 10000 iterations before returning -ETIMEDOUT.

This also corrects the previous commit's own prediction on the record.
Landing the WAN port without this GPHY does not merely leave WAN dark.
of_mdiobus_register tolerates a PHY that does not answer and registers
the bus without it, so no phy_device is created and
phylink_of_phy_connect -> phylink_fwnode_phy_connect returns -ENODEV
(phylink.c:2301-2302) rather than -EPROBE_DEFER. Probe requires every
declared port to come up, so the failure rolls back all five netdevs and
the board has no ethernet at all.

dtc: all three boards build; the 7590 builds warning-free and the fifth
entry is present in the DTB. The 7560 and easy350_anywan are untouched -
neither includes this file.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
index dc1eeaa54a41..119ee3ad01f9 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
@@ -163,31 +163,45 @@ partition@1500000 {
 };
 
 /*
- * The four LAN GPHYs, which are the same four the FRITZ!Box 7560 has: silicon ids
- * 0x02..0x05 on RCU reset bits 29 / 28 / 26 / 25, in that order.
+ * All five GPHYs. The first four are the ones the FRITZ!Box 7560 also has -- silicon
+ * ids 0x02..0x05 on RCU reset bits 29 / 28 / 26 / 25 -- and the fifth, silicon
+ * id 0x0F on bit 31, is this board's WAN PHY. The bits are non-contiguous and
+ * out of order: 29, 28, 26, 25, 31, with 27 unused. No closed form fits them,
+ * which is why every entry is spelled out.
  *
- * AVM's own DTS gives the fifth reset as bit 31, but bit 31 was found on the
- * GRX350 to be the GPHY-GROUP MASTER reset rather than a per-PHY GE reset --
- * toggling it toppled the clock group the four GE cores had just booted from,
- * and they reverted to ROM.
+ * That reading does not carry over to the GRX550. The vendor's own device
+ * tree names bit 31 "phy4" -- the fifth GPHY's individual reset line --
+ * alongside 29/28/26/25 for phy0..phy3 and bit 30 for the global chip reset,
+ * and its console boots all five GPHYs from this one GSW-L-side block.
  *
- * Adding the fifth PHY is one appended entry in each of these three
- * properties: phy_id 0x0F, reset-name "phyF", and its reset cell.
+ * The second cell is the RST_STAT status bit. It is inert on this path: the
+ * firmware loader takes the reset_control handle only so the named-line
+ * lookup succeeds and then discards it outright -- xrx500_phy_fw.c:257,
+ * "(void)phy_rst" -- and drives RCU_RST_REQ by direct read-modify-write
+ * instead (:297 assert, :322 deassert), with the bit coming from its own
+ * xrx500_gphy[] table rather than from the DT. Neither cell ever reaches the
+ * provider's .status op, which is the only thing in the reset framework that
+ * reads the status register. Both cells must still clear the provider's "<
+ * nr_resets" bounds check, which 31 does.
  *
- * ⚠ Until it is added THIS BOARD HAS NO ETHERNET AT ALL, not merely no WAN.
- * The WAN port declared above hard-requires its PHY: with no firmware the PHY
- * does not answer MDIO, phylink_of_phy_connect returns -ENODEV rather than
- * deferring, and the driver requires every declared port to come up, so the
- * four working LAN netdevs are rolled back with it. Land the fifth GPHY
- * before booting this board.
+ * The aperture in seale_avm.dtsi is already sized for five: GPHYF's LBADR and
+ * MBADR sit at offsets 0x268 and 0x26C, inside reg = <0x1c003c00 0x280>.
+ *
+ * All five entries are load-bearing for every port, not just the WAN one. A
+ * PHY with no firmware does not answer MDIO; of_mdiobus_register tolerates
+ * that and brings the bus up without it, but no phy_device is created, so
+ * phylink_of_phy_connect -> phylink_fwnode_phy_connect returns -ENODEV rather
+ * than -EPROBE_DEFER. Probe requires every declared port to come up, so that
+ * one failure rolls the four working LAN netdevs back with it.
  */
 &gphy_fw {
-	phy_id = <0x02 0x03 0x04 0x05>;
+	phy_id = <0x02 0x03 0x04 0x05 0x0F>;
 	resets = <&rcu_reset0 29 29>,
 		 <&rcu_reset0 28 28>,
 		 <&rcu_reset0 26 26>,
-		 <&rcu_reset0 25 25>;
-	reset-names = "phy2", "phy3", "phy4", "phy5";
+		 <&rcu_reset0 25 25>,
+		 <&rcu_reset0 31 31>;
+	reset-names = "phy2", "phy3", "phy4", "phy5", "phyF";
 };
 
